---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import Prose from '../components/Prose.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');

// Condensed from the "Not qualified" table in docs/roadmap.md, in plainer words.
// Keep its distinction between what a test measured and what a person reported:
// that distinction is the point of the page.
const notQualified = [
  { area: 'Public distribution, code signing, ARM64', status: 'Not started.' },
  { area: 'Clean-machine install', status: 'Reported as passing by the author, on a machine since used for everything else. There is no automated test and none is planned; accepted as a limitation on 2026-09-19.' },
  { area: 'Installed startup on large datasets', status: 'Measured, and accepted as an exception rather than a pass. Opening a file inspects it twice, and the layout signature scan is the largest single cost.' },
  { area: 'Human usability and accessibility', status: 'Reported by the author. Scaling, keyboard and focus, and screen-reader checks were done by hand, not measured. Accepted as a limitation on 2026-09-20.' },
  { area: 'Cloud sync and live-root writes', status: 'Unsupported. Nendo warns about known sync folders but makes no claim that syncing is safe.' },
  { area: 'Physical power loss', status: 'Not tested.' },
  { area: 'Broad MCP client parity', status: 'Not promised. Claude Code and Codex both connect with just the address, checked by optional tests against installed clients. Other clients are untested.' },
  { area: 'Account boundary on a shared machine', status: 'Removed along with the password, to move faster on a single-user machine. There is no plan to restore it.' },
  { area: 'Cross-platform', status: 'Windows only.' },
  { area: 'The notification area and Windows notifications', status: 'Reported by the author. A test checks that closing hides the window, that the process and its file stay open, and that Exit quits. The icon, its menu and the notifications themselves cannot be checked from a script.' },
  { area: 'What the Windows shell draws', status: 'Reported by the author, item by item. Registry writes, shell identity and the Jump List file are tested. Whether Explorer shows the file icon, the taskbar shows the badge or progress, or a file dragged from Explorer arrives cannot be checked from a script.' },
];

const delivered = [
  { slice: 'S0', body: 'A colour for every choice option, and a record-page header.' },
  { slice: 'S1', body: 'Exact grouped totals, a breakdown chart, a progress ring, and click-through to a filtered list.' },
  { slice: 'S2', body: 'A gallery of cards, and a rating shown on a fixed scale.' },
  { slice: 'S3', body: 'The timeline: records placed by a Date field, with an optional end date drawn as a span.' },
  { slice: 'S4', body: 'The overview page, which belongs to the whole file rather than to one record type.' },
  { slice: 'S5', body: 'The trend chart and the activity grid, grouped over a date range.' },
  { slice: 'S6', body: 'The matrix, which crosses two choice fields in a single query, and the ranked list.' },
  { slice: 'S7', body: 'A board grouped by a reference field, so each column is a record of another type.' },
];

const gaps = [
  'Consent is shown where you accept a change, but not in the proposal queue.',
  'Validation reports only one error at a time.',
  'Overlapping proposals are flagged, but not rebased onto each other.',
  'A reconnecting agent can see a pending proposal but cannot act on it.',
  'With Unattended authoring, nobody reviews a change before it is applied, and nothing records that it went in unreviewed.',
  'Imports are not atomic. They are saved fifty rows per revision.',
  'Any program on this computer can reach an open file. There is no password.',
  'The calendar shows a Date field on a month. There are no times, time zones, week or day views, durations, recurring events or drag-to-reschedule.',
  'A rating scale is fixed once set, and a timeline shows one calendar year at a time.',
  'The selected tab and screen are not saved, so reopening a file returns every tab group to its first tab.',
  'A whole-file snapshot with calculations grows linearly with size: 49 ms cold at 1,000 records, 474 ms at 10,000.',
];
---

<Page
  title="Status — Nendo"
  description="What works, what has shipped, what has been measured, what rests on the author's own testing, and what is not supported at all."
  eyebrow="Status"
  heading="Where Nendo stands."
  lede="This project keeps a strict line between what an automated test measured and what a person reported. This page follows the same rule."
>
  <section class="page section">
    <div class="panel rule">
      <p class="eyebrow">The rule</p>
      <p class="rule-quote">
        Say what was checked, not whether something is “verified”. “Verified” and
        “unverified” collapse several different questions into one word and read as a
        verdict on quality, which is usually not what is meant. Name the lane and its
        scope instead.
      </p>
      <p class="rule-source">
        — from the repository's contributor guide, which this site follows too.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="page section">
    <p class="eyebrow">Where it is</p>
    <h2>Version 0.12.0: the core loop works.</h2>
    <Prose>
      <p>
        The core loop works end to end, as an unsigned per-user install for Windows x64.
        Four reference applications, each a different shape, put the idea to the test:
        Idea Garden, Decision Log, the Axiom Register and Nendo Station. The last two were
        built from an empty file by an agent working only through MCP, which learned what
        it could build from the interface rather than from the source.
      </p>
      <p>
        Nendo's own development is planned in a Nendo file, used every day by two coding
        agents that hand work to each other. It has four record types (initiatives, work
        items, findings and checks), twenty-one screens and commands, five calculations,
        one reusable function, one action and one trigger. Each check records how it was
        made (automated, observed by an agent, or reported by the author) separately from
        its result, and a passing check never marks work done on its own.
      </p>
      <p>
        The product has <strong>no CI</strong>, by choice. The local test gate
        (<code>Test-Repository.ps1</code>, <code>Test-Production.ps1</code>) takes about a
        minute and is the only gate. The repository's one GitHub Actions workflow builds
        and deploys this website and nothing else.
      </p>
    </Prose>
  </section>

  <section class="page section">
    <p class="eyebrow">Delivered</p>
    <h2>Screen features, in the order they shipped.</h2>
    <ul class="slices">
      {delivered.map(item => (
        <li><code>{item.slice}</code><span>{item.body}</span></li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <p class="aside">
      Calculations, reusable functions, local actions and automatic triggers have
      shipped. Custom views are approved and being built; approval does not count as
      passing the checks below that have not run yet.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="page section">
    <p class="eyebrow">Not qualified</p>
    <h2>What Nendo does not claim yet.</h2>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th style="width: 30%">Area</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {notQualified.map(row => (
            <tr><td><strong>{row.area}</strong></td><td>{row.status}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="aside">
      <a href={`${base}/docs/roadmap`}>The roadmap</a> has the full table, with every
      measurement.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="page section">
    <p class="eyebrow">Known gaps</p>
    <h2>Where the working features stop.</h2>
    <ul class="gaps">{gaps.map(gap => <li>{gap}</li>)}</ul>
  </section>

  <section class="page section">
    <div class="panel risk">
      <p class="eyebrow">The biggest risk</p>
      <h2>Everyone who has used Nendo built it.</h2>
      <p>
        Every usability, scaling and accessibility result so far comes from the author.
        That says something about the product, but nothing about whether it makes sense
        to someone coming to it fresh.
      </p>
      <p>
        The only way to find out is for other people to use it. The Idea Garden journey
        walks through the whole core loop and is the place to start. If you get lost along
        the way, that is exactly what the project needs to hear.
      </p>
      <p class="risk-cta">
        <a class="button button-primary" href="https://github.com/ThomasRohde/nendo/issues" rel="noopener">Report what confused you</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Page>

<style>
  .rule { border-left: 3px solid var(--violet); }
  .rule-quote {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: clamp(1.1rem, 2.2vw, 1.4rem);
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    line-height: 1.45;
    max-width: 58ch;
  }
  .rule-source { margin: 0; color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.88rem; }

  .slices {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 24px 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 9px;
    max-width: 78ch;
  }

  .slices li {
    display: flex;
    gap: 14px;
    align-items: baseline;
    padding: 12px 18px;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--surface-raised);
  }

  .slices code {
    flex: none;
    background: var(--cobalt-soft);
    border-color: transparent;
    color: var(--cobalt);
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  .slices span { font-size: 0.94rem; }

  .aside {
    margin-top: 22px;
    max-width: 68ch;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.94rem;
  }

  .table-scroll {
    margin-top: 24px;
    overflow-x: auto;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--surface-raised);
  }

  .table-scroll table { margin: 0; }
  .table-scroll tr:last-child td { border-bottom: 0; }
  .table-scroll td { font-size: 0.92rem; }

  .gaps {
    margin: 24px 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    display: grid;
    gap: 8px;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(340px, 1fr));
  }

  .gaps li {
    padding: 13px 17px;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--warning);
    border-radius: 0 var(--radius) var(--radius) 0;
    background: var(--surface-soft);
    font-size: 0.92rem;
  }

  .risk h2 { max-width: 22ch; }
  .risk p { max-width: 62ch; }
  .risk-cta { margin-top: 22px; }
</style>
